7.17.m:
[They saw the commanders of the hosts,] with red hair, yellow beards, shaven heads, others sky-clad, / wearing matted locks, tawny-eyed, and girdled with serpents, // heroic, wearing snakes as armlets, wearing earrings of hissing serpent kings, / with limbs smeared with purifying ashes, pale as the moon,
dancing and leaping about, their eyes wide open with laughter, / the commanders of the hosts surrounded by numerous other troops: // with faces of tortoises and roosters, with faces of hares, monitor lizards, and wolves, / terrible, with faces of donkeys, camels, and horses, and with faces of boars and buffaloes;
with faces of men and rams, with faces of dogs, jackals, and deer, / terrible, with faces of sea-monsters, and with faces resembling enraged porpoises, / with faces of cats and wolves, with long faces, and faceless;

with faces resembling cuckoos, owls, ruddy shelducks, and woodpeckers, / with faces like those of mice, mongooses, and cats, invincible to enemies, // and others with faces of goats and rams, with faces of parrots and dabchicks, / with faces of bears and tigers, and likewise with faces of leopards and lions;
terrible, with faces of elephants, and likewise with faces of hyenas, / with faces of Garuḍas and jackals, and with faces of crows and cuckoos, // and others with faces of bulls and monkeys, and likewise with faces of hawks and vultures, / with huge bellies, feet, and limbs, starry-eyed, endowed with great strength;
and others with faces of pigeons, and likewise with faces of roosters, / with faces of quails and partridges, and even with faces of chameleons;

[…]
with three crests, with two crests, and also with seven crests, / crested, with long matted hair, shaven-headed, and with tawny hair;
with variegated eyes, variegated faces, anointed with diverse garlands and pastes, / wearing variegated garments, heroic, ever fond of battle, // with black, fleshless faces, long backs, and thin bellies, / with broad backs, huge necks, pot-bellied and large-bellied;

with huge arms, huge faces, short necks, and lean faces, / hunchbacked, with long shanks, and necks like elephants' ears, // with long lips, long tongues, long eyes, and long noses, / with huge fangs, good fangs, variegated fangs, and of great strength, // with well-proportioned bodies, radiant, well-adorned, / tawny-eyed, with large ears, spike-eared, and earless;
long-fanged, broad-chested, thick-lipped, with tawny hair, / with diverse feet, lips, and fangs, with diverse hands and necks;
covered with diverse skins, draped with diverse garments, / rejoicing, rushing around, dancing, making great roars;
pendulous-eared, broad-chested, blue-throated, three-eyed, / some resembling a parrot's belly, some resembling collyrium;

870 / Then the Mountain-Born Lady bowed to the Supreme Lord and said: / 'Because I am your companion in righteousness and your dearly beloved, / and attained by ancient love, therefore I ask without hesitation. / O Granter of Desires, your purifying, holy names, / endowed with their meanings, I wish to hear explained by you, O omnipresent Lord.' / Thus questioned then by Gaurī, the sovereign of the gods declared everything.
'The illumination of the meanings of [my] names is equal to the fruits of all sacrifices; / it is my supreme secret, O goddess, which Brahmā and the others know. / That I shall tell you, O goddess, by which divinity is attained. / Because I, all-pervading, drive away diseases in the world,
and because I destroy weeping, therefore, O beloved, I am Rudra. / For at the time of dissolution, I withdraw the entire universe;
and since I am also the remover of death, for that reason I am proclaimed as Hara. / The body of all living beings here is declared to be a city (pura);

Where all the gods, O Vyāsa, led by the Four-faced One [Brahmā],
and perfected sages, as well as those who are lords of yoga on earth,
having worshipped Hara with devotion, attained the highest abode;
where Mahādeva directly placed his own foot;
Bowing to whom, all men are freed from all impurities,
having approached that Īśāna who dwells in Mahālaya;
Where, having cast off the body, no matter how or where,
a mortal is not born again, having attained the unsurpassable perfection;
Men who are ignorant of dharma or who commit sins do not behold him;
there stands the mighty Mahāgaṇapati stationed at the gateway;

Even there, the Lord himself is always directly present, O sage;
having approached that Īśāna, one does not grieve over death.
Among the regions of the mountain Himavat, even across all three worlds,
this one is the most supreme, O Vyāsa, auspicious and granting all perfections.
Merely by approaching it, the fruit of all sacrifices is said to be gained;
for one who perishes or through mishap, the excellent destination is the rank of a gaṇa lord.
Nothing holier than this is found in all the worlds:
sovereignty over the three worlds, or death on this mountain;
Death here is superior even to the status of a universal monarch;
endowed with supreme sovereignty, one attains union with Śambhu.
884
A creature having died on this mountain, what higher abode could there be?
Yet there is another great peak of the Himavat frequented by perfected beings,

899
Well-fitted with charming gates, balconies, latticed windows, and golden streets and courtyards;
with mansions whose floors were touched by moonbeams, providing pleasant, delightful ingress for the cool breeze;
with pearls, corals, and varied gem-fragments hanging down, blazing like strange lightning;
and with variegated banners tossed by the wind and fluttering flags, it shone exceedingly.
With gem-fragments set in rows of palaces, resembling the luster of great sapphire-kings,
and with the tops of lofty mansions adorned with archways, that Tripura shone resplendent.
Surrounded on all sides by exceedingly tall trees, fruitful and blossoming with flowers of all seasons, ..

and endowed with hundreds of delightful gardens where bees, peacocks, and swans wandered;
with step-wells crowded with birds and covered with blossoming blue water lilies,
and likewise with lakes where dancing peacock birds called, that abode of the Daityas was magnificent;
and it shone exceedingly, crowded with women whose breasts bore nail-marks, whose eyes had red corners reaching to their ears,
and whose glances were modest and slightly tremulous.
Having made that city of iron, Maya assigned it below, giving it to Tārakākṣa;
the second city, which was of silver, [he gave] to Vidyutprabha, the lordly king of Asuras;
and the third, divine one of gold, king Maya took for his own sake. ..

Occupied by those three lion-like Danu kings, Tripura then flourished equally.
Having conquered their heavenly realms on all sides, accompanied by their beloved wives, friends, and servants,
they enjoyed various pleasures in it, taking no thought for the concerns of the three worlds.
Their passions bound by pride and delusion, they considered the gods inferior in strength;
and thinking thus, they attacked forcefully and inflicted many kinds of extreme torments [upon them].
They reduced gardens such as the Vaibhrāja forest to broken creepers and flowers;
they made the step-wells devoid of lotuses, and abducted Rambhā and other celestial women;
those women of the gods, their limbs trembling with fright, and other [deities] were subjected by them to great torment. ..
